 "name": "Green Office Restorativeness: IRCS Ratings of Office Photos",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Shibata, Tokuhiro, Ikeuchi, Ito, Kaji & Muramatsu (2024), \"Visual properties and perceived restorativeness in green offices\", Frontiers in Psychology 15: 1443540. Participants see five office photographs, from bare to heavily planted, one at a time, imagine being in each room and rate the 11-item Indoor Restorative Characteristic Scale (IRCS: Being Away, Fascination, Extent) from 1 = not at all to 5 = very much. Items are the authors' English wording from Supplementary Data Sheet 1, shown in a random order kept for each participant. Photographs are the twelve printed in the paper's Figure 1 (eight Study 1 offices) and Figure 3 (four Study 2 samples), CC BY 4.0. Scoring: subscale means with the two Extent items \"cramped\" and \"stifling\" reversed; overall restorativeness is the mean of the three subscales; compare scores with the share of green in each photo. Differences from the original study: (1) twelve printed photos instead of Study 2's sixty, and five are drawn at random rather than from greenery-balanced groups; (2) photos are cropped from the figures, about 485 px wide; (3) one instructed attention check item instead of two; (4) English items instead of the Japanese originals.",
